# Achievable-score lattice: how finely can ANY zensim-targeting method land?
#
# date          2026-08-06
# host          Darwin 26.5.2 (macOS), Apple aarch64, 12 cores, 24 GiB RAM
# rustc         rustc 1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14)
# zenavif       9a1c7626 fix(two-pass-zensim): drop the cross-metric content seed
# zensim        git imazen/zensim main @ e4b875b5 (0.3.0), profile codec_target() == B
# encoder       zenravif path dep ../ravif/ravif, speed 6, threads=1
# NOT built with -C target-cpu=native
#
# command
#   ./target/release/examples/zensim_loop_bench sweep <2-image manifest> 6 256 \
#       50,51,52,...,80          (every INTEGER quality in 50..80)
#   manifest: clic2025/final-test/937476dd... (photo) + gb82-sc/imessage (screen)
#
# why           zenavif's `quality` resolves to an INTEGER AV1 quantizer index,
#               so the set of achievable zensim scores for a given image is a
#               discrete lattice. Any targeting loop -- secant, closed loop, or
#               a perfect oracle -- can only land ON that lattice. This file
#               measures its spacing so convergence tolerances can be set to
#               something the codec can actually deliver.
#
# result        Over quality 50..80 all 31 integer qualities resolve to 31
#               DISTINCT quantizer indices (the mapping is 1:1 in this band),
#               and the zensim gap between adjacent achievable scores is:
#                 photo  (937476dd): min 0.100  median 1.052  max 2.668
#                 screen (imessage): min 0.016  median 0.816  max 4.155
#               53% / 47% of adjacent gaps EXCEED 1.0, so a +-0.5 tolerance
#               band contains no achievable score at all about half the time.
#               A +-0.5 target is therefore unreachable ~50% of the time for
#               reasons that have nothing to do with the search.
#
# columns       image size w h q qindex bytes zensim dm_mean enc_ms
#
# scheduling    Run under `nice -n 5`, NOT `nice -n 19`. macOS maps high nice
#               values onto the background QoS class, confining the process to
#               efficiency cores: measured 2026-08-06 on an idle box, the same
#               workload got ~17% of one core at nice 19 vs ~145% at nice 5
#               (~40x wall clock). nice 5 still yields to interactive work.
